  <content>
    <section>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="fc849925-6fe7-4a85-8c37-8e6a82941051" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For questions 1 - 5, use the the three exponential functions defined below:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=100 \cdot 3^x\)</para>
            <para>\(g(x)=100 \cdot (3.5)^x\)</para>
            <para>\(h(x)=100 \cdot 4^x\)</para>
            <para> Which of these functions grows the least quickly? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(f(x)\). The value of \(f\) triples each time \(x\) increases by 1 because the growth factor (or the base) is 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_68b4e6eb-d822-4062-8daa-2bb942ca1943" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which function grows the most quickly?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(h(x)\). The value of \(h\) quadruples each time \(x\) increases by 1 because the growth factor (or the base) is 4.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fefa6cad-3510-4b34-bb22-a3c8f83ab3f6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The three given graphs represent the functions \(f\), \(g\), and \(h\) defined above.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_10_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Which graph corresponds to \(f(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph C.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_26ae9706-ccab-4825-92a7-7fd4c05d0223" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph corresponds to \(g(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7fdbee7a-0aea-460f-ae0d-ff8702853a51" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph corresponds to \(h(x)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph A.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_65995a95-e6ea-40fa-a207-2ec65cb4d679" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For questions 6 - 8, use the given graphs of three exponential equations.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_10_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Match each equation with its graph.</para>
            <para>Which graph matches the equation \(y=20 \cdot 3^x\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.12.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph M.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7f927dd8-8ee6-46c0-8e0c-419eb84279ad" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph matches the equation \(y=50 \cdot 3^x\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.12.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph L.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_93bf12cc-1d12-42da-95a4-6bec93e2bd2e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph matches the equation \(y=100 \cdot 3^x\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.12.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph K.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_69c014bc-c939-4181-9282-4f5f66d0e9e5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For questions 9 - 10, use the graph below.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_10_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para> Written in the form \(f(x)=a \cdot b^x\), what is the value of \(a\)<emphasis> </emphasis>for each function?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.3. 
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 160.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3781b9cd-ca4c-4fe0-81eb-5dffa0788c7c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which graph decreases the most quickly?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph C.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_899336df-b768-443d-b6de-9feb1fd0e752" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The graphs of \(y=50 \cdot 2^x\), \(y=200 \cdot 2^x\), and \(y=100 \cdot 2^x\) are shown.</para>
            <para>Which is the graph of \(y=100 \cdot 2^x\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.12.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d6125db6-2ebc-469e-a89d-2be4e6a30b80" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For questions 12 – 14, use the graph below:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Graph of six exponential functions.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_10_04.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Which of the functions graphed, \(y=a \cdot b^x\), has the smallest value for \(a\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.12.3 – 5.12.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is F. Since F has the lowest \(y\)-intercept, it has the smallest value of \(a\).

     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_47391196-f897-4c2a-af5f-a545678afce2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which of the functions graphed, \(y=a \cdot b^x\), has the lowest value for \(b\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.2. 
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is C. Since C is decaying the fastest, it has the lowest value for \(b\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="af7ac488-3fd7-429e-aafc-f9a4fdf1d9ca" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the equation of the horizontal asymptote for \(f(x)=5 \cdot 2^x-3\)?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=2\)</para>
            <para>\(y=3\)</para>
            <para>\(y=-3\)</para>
            <para>\(y=5\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.12.2. 
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(y= -3\). The –3 in the function shifts the exponential down 3.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
